{"stage": "step:s3", "served_model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4088, "output_tokens": 466, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "content": [{"text": "I ran the significance grid for all three strategies at 20%, 35%, 50%, 65% and 80% signal efficiency (15 Monte Carlo runs, 100,000 events each) and recorded the results.\n\n| Strategy | 20% | 35% | 50% | 65% | 80% |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| S1 | 2.262 | 2.422 | 2.396 | 2.667 | 2.233 |\n| S2 | 2.824 | 3.402 | 3.434 | 3.400 | 2.887 |\n| S3 | 3.516 | 3.884 | 3.882 | 3.890 | 3.509 |\n\nSelection-S3 gives the highest expected significance at every working point, peaking at about 3.89\u03c3 near 65% efficiency. Its plateau runs from 35% to 65%.\n\nHowever, the earlier validation result shows S3 has a 35% validation-region yield disagreement, against a 10% tolerance. S2 (6%) and S1 (3%) pass. The brief requires a robust background estimate, so S3's higher significance may not be trustworthy. The significance numbers here are statistical-only, so they do not capture this background-modelling problem.\n\nThe best robust candidate is S2 at about 50% efficiency (3.43\u03c3), which passes validation and is close to S3's peak. The final recommendation belongs to a later step, so I have not chosen a strategy yet.", "type": "text"}]}
